WEBVTT

NOTE
Draft cues converted from captions.srt. Final wording and timing are recorded later.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:23.850 line:95% align:center size:80%
I build this tree with the seams-core pixi environment. The two lines on screen are the install: meson setup build, then meson compile -C build. That produces the seams command and libseams, which is the library the C++ call sits on. I keep that build and use it for every count in this recording.

2
00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:39.470 line:95% align:center size:80%
The input is input/traj/ice1h.lammpstrj. seams read prints nop 128 frame 1. Those are the 128 oxygens in one frame. This is the cell docs_engine_openmm scores, and it is the cell I hand to C++.

3
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:57.320 line:95% align:center size:80%
Neighbours are a cutoff of 3.5 angstroms on type 1. seams cn prints nI 128, so every oxygen is in the list. Ice Ih keeps a four-neighbour shell inside that cutoff. The C++ neighbour call walks this same list.

4
00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:12.890 line:95% align:center size:80%
Primitive rings use that list. The histogram is 6 256, and the summary is rings 256. Two six-rings for each of the 128 waters, and no other size. docs_engine_openmm names that count six-rings 256.

5
00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:29.020 line:95% align:center size:80%
Local q6 on the same cutoff prints q6 0.625855. Published CHILL+ then prints hexagonal 128. Every water is hexagonal. I want the C++ result to show the same two figures before I trust the wrapper.

6
00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:50.250 line:95% align:center size:80%
The C++ program is docs/videos/cpp/ice.cpp, through seams_lang.h. seams_frame_read loads the dump, and the calls are neighbours, steinhardt on the cutoff graph, chill_plus, and rings. The line is nop 128 pairs 512 q6 0.625855 hexagonal 128 rings 256. pairs 512 is the directed neighbour count, four per water.
